export default {"T000400":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"equivalence\" data-relation-style=\"equivalence\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Equivalence</span><h3>Weak dimension at most one ⇔ Valuation ring at every prime</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Left conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000085\"><span>Weak dimension at most one</span><code>P000085</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000400\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000400</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇔</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Right conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000087\"><span>Valuation ring at every prime</span><code>P000087</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> Use the ideal-flatness criterion and the classification of the local domains whose finitely generated ideals are principal.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/092S\">Stacks 092S · weak dimension at most one</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/more-algebra/#tag-092S\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000400\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000400\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000401":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Absolutely flat ring ⇒ Weak dimension at most one</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000084\"><span>Absolutely flat ring</span><code>P000084</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000401\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000401</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000085\"><span>Weak dimension at most one</span><code>P000085</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> Flat dimension zero is at most one.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/092D\">Stacks 092D · weak dimension</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/more-algebra/#tag-092D\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/092F\">Stacks 092F · absolute flatness criterion</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/more-algebra/#tag-092F\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000401\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000401\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000402":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"equivalence\" data-relation-style=\"equivalence\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Equivalence</span><h3>Local ring ∧ Weak dimension at most one ⇔ Valuation ring</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Left conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000080\"><span>Local ring</span><code>P000080</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000085\"><span>Weak dimension at most one</span><code>P000085</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000402\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000402</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇔</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Right conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000106\"><span>Valuation ring</span><code>P000106</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> A local ring equals its localization at the maximal ideal. Localizations of valuation rings are valuation rings.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/092S\">Stacks 092S · weak dimension at most one</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/more-algebra/#tag-092S\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00I9\">Stacks 00I9 · valuation rings</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00I9\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000402\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000402\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000403":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Absolutely flat ring ⇒ Normal ring</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000084\"><span>Absolutely flat ring</span><code>P000084</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000403\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000403</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000101\"><span>Normal ring</span><code>P000101</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> Each prime localization is a field, and each field is an integrally closed domain.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/092F\">Stacks 092F · field localizations</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/more-algebra/#tag-092F\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00GV\">Stacks 00GV · normality at primes</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00GV\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000403\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000403\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000404":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Noetherian ring ⇒ Coherent ring</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000088\"><span>Noetherian ring</span><code>P000088</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000404\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000404</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000091\"><span>Coherent ring</span><code>P000091</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> Finitely generated modules over a Noetherian ring are finitely presented, including finitely generated ideals.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/05CY\">Stacks 05CY · Noetherian implies coherent</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-05CY\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000404\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000404\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000405":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Noetherian ring ⇒ Finitely many minimal primes</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000088\"><span>Noetherian ring</span><code>P000088</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000405\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000405</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000092\"><span>Finitely many minimal primes</span><code>P000092</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> The spectrum of a Noetherian ring is a Noetherian space. Its irreducible components correspond to minimal primes and are finite in number.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00FR\">Stacks 00FR · finite set of minimal primes</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00FR\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000405\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000405\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000406":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"equivalence\" data-relation-style=\"equivalence\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Equivalence</span><h3>Noetherian ring ∧ Krull dimension at most zero ⇔ Artinian ring</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Left conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000088\"><span>Noetherian ring</span><code>P000088</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000094\"><span>Krull dimension at most zero</span><code>P000094</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000406\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000406</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇔</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Right conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000089\"><span>Artinian ring</span><code>P000089</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> A Noetherian ring with every prime maximal is Artinian. Conversely an Artinian ring is Noetherian and every prime is maximal.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00JB\">Stacks 00JB · Artinian rings and length</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00JB\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00KH\">Stacks 00KH · Artinian dimension criterion</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00KH\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00KJ\">Stacks 00KJ · equivalent characterizations</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00KJ\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000406\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000406\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000407":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"equivalence\" data-relation-style=\"equivalence\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Equivalence</span><h3>Artinian ring ⇔ Finite length over itself</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Left conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000089\"><span>Artinian ring</span><code>P000089</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000407\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000407</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇔</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Right conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000090\"><span>Finite length over itself</span><code>P000090</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> The Artinian finite-length criterion applies to the ring as a module over itself.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00JB\">Stacks 00JB · Artinian rings and length</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00JB\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000407\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000407\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000408":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Artinian ring ⇒ Finitely many prime ideals</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000089\"><span>Artinian ring</span><code>P000089</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000408\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000408</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000093\"><span>Finitely many prime ideals</span><code>P000093</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> Every prime is maximal and the set of maximal ideals is finite.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00J7\">Stacks 00J7 · finitely many maximal ideals</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00J7\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00JB\">Stacks 00JB · every prime maximal</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00JB\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000408\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000408\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000409":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"equivalence\" data-relation-style=\"equivalence\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Equivalence</span><h3>Artinian ring ∧ Reduced ring ⇔ Finite product of fields</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Left conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000089\"><span>Artinian ring</span><code>P000089</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000079\"><span>Reduced ring</span><code>P000079</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000409\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000409</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇔</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Right conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000105\"><span>Finite product of fields</span><code>P000105</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> Artinian rings decompose as a finite product of their maximal localizations. If reduced, each localization is a field. A finite product of fields is Artinian and reduced.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00JB\">Stacks 00JB · Artinian product decomposition</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00JB\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/092F\">Stacks 092F · field localizations</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/more-algebra/#tag-092F\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000409\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000409\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000410":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Finitely many prime ideals ∧ Noetherian ring ⇒ Krull dimension at most one</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000093\"><span>Finitely many prime ideals</span><code>P000093</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000088\"><span>Noetherian ring</span><code>P000088</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000410\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000410</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000095\"><span>Krull dimension at most one</span><code>P000095</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> A local Noetherian domain of dimension at least two has infinitely many prime ideals. The theorem extends the conclusion by passing to minimal-prime quotients and maximal localizations.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0ALV\">Stacks 0ALV · finite prime spectrum of a Noetherian ring</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-0ALV\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000410\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000410\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000411":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Krull dimension at most zero ⇒ Krull dimension at most one</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000094\"><span>Krull dimension at most zero</span><code>P000094</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000411\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000411</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000095\"><span>Krull dimension at most one</span><code>P000095</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> A chain with two strict inclusions would have a strict inclusion.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0GIE\">Stacks 0GIE · chains of prime ideals</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-0GIE\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00KE\">Stacks 00KE · Krull dimension</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00KE\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00KG\">Stacks 00KG · Krull dimension and prime heights</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00KG\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000411\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000411\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000412":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Krull dimension exactly one ⇒ Krull dimension at most one</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000096\"><span>Krull dimension exactly one</span><code>P000096</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000412\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000412</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000095\"><span>Krull dimension at most one</span><code>P000095</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> The definition bounds the length of any chain by one.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0GIE\">Stacks 0GIE · chains of prime ideals</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-0GIE\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00KE\">Stacks 00KE · Krull dimension</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00KE\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00KG\">Stacks 00KG · Krull dimension and prime heights</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00KG\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000412\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000412\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000413":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Krull dimension exactly one ⇒ Nonzero ring</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000096\"><span>Krull dimension exactly one</span><code>P000096</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000413\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000413</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000076\"><span>Nonzero ring</span><code>P000076</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> A prime exists if a strict inclusion of primes occurs.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00AQ\">Stacks 00AQ · zero-ring convention</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00AQ\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0GIE\">Stacks 0GIE · chains of prime ideals</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-0GIE\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00KE\">Stacks 00KE · Krull dimension</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00KE\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00KG\">Stacks 00KG · Krull dimension and prime heights</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00KG\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000413\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000413\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000414":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Krull dimension at most one ⇒ Finite Krull dimension</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000095\"><span>Krull dimension at most one</span><code>P000095</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000414\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000414</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000097\"><span>Finite Krull dimension</span><code>P000097</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> The common finite bound is one.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0GIE\">Stacks 0GIE · chains of prime ideals</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-0GIE\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00KE\">Stacks 00KE · Krull dimension</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00KE\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00KG\">Stacks 00KG · Krull dimension and prime heights</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00KG\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000414\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000414\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000415":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Krull dimension at most one ⇒ Catenary ring</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000095\"><span>Krull dimension at most one</span><code>P000095</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000415\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000415</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000099\"><span>Catenary ring</span><code>P000099</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> Equal endpoints give length zero. Distinct comparable endpoints admit only a chain of length one between them.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00NI\">Stacks 00NI · catenary rings</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00NI\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0GIE\">Stacks 0GIE · chains of prime ideals</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-0GIE\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00KE\">Stacks 00KE · Krull dimension</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00KE\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00KG\">Stacks 00KG · Krull dimension and prime heights</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00KG\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000415\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000415\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000416":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Universally catenary ring ⇒ Noetherian ring ∧ Catenary ring</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000100\"><span>Universally catenary ring</span><code>P000100</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000416\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000416</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000088\"><span>Noetherian ring</span><code>P000088</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000099\"><span>Catenary ring</span><code>P000099</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> Noetherianity is part of the definition, and the ring is of finite type over itself.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00NL\">Stacks 00NL · universally catenary ring</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00NL\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000416\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000416\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000417":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Normal ring ⇒ Reduced ring</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000101\"><span>Normal ring</span><code>P000101</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000417\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000417</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000079\"><span>Reduced ring</span><code>P000079</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> Every nilpotent maps to zero in each prime localization, and prime localizations detect a zero element.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00GV\">Stacks 00GV · normal rings</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00GV\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00HN\">Stacks 00HN · local detection of zero</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00HN\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000417\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000417\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000418":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Normal ring ⇒ Integrally closed in the total ring of fractions</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000101\"><span>Normal ring</span><code>P000101</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000418\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000418</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000103\"><span>Integrally closed in the total ring of fractions</span><code>P000103</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> An element integral over the ring lies in every prime localization. Local detection of membership in the ring then applies.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/034M\">Stacks 034M · normal rings are integrally closed in their total ring of fractions</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-034M\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000418\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000418\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000419":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"equivalence\" data-relation-style=\"equivalence\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Equivalence</span><h3>Integral domain ∧ Normal ring ⇔ Normal domain</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Left conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000078\"><span>Integral domain</span><code>P000078</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000101\"><span>Normal ring</span><code>P000101</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000419\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000419</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇔</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Right conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000102\"><span>Normal domain</span><code>P000102</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> For a domain, normality can be checked at the maximal ideals or at all primes.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/030B\">Stacks 030B · normality of a domain is local</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-030B\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000419\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000419\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000420":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"equivalence\" data-relation-style=\"equivalence\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Equivalence</span><h3>Integral domain ∧ Integrally closed in the total ring of fractions ⇔ Normal domain</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Left conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000078\"><span>Integral domain</span><code>P000078</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000103\"><span>Integrally closed in the total ring of fractions</span><code>P000103</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000420\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000420</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇔</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Right conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000102\"><span>Normal domain</span><code>P000102</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> The total ring of fractions of a domain equals its fraction field.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0309\">Stacks 0309 · normal domain</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-0309\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000420\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000420\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000421":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Reduced ring ∧ Finitely many minimal primes ∧ Integrally closed in the total ring of fractions ⇒ Normal ring</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000079\"><span>Reduced ring</span><code>P000079</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000092\"><span>Finitely many minimal primes</span><code>P000092</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000103\"><span>Integrally closed in the total ring of fractions</span><code>P000103</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000421\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000421</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000101\"><span>Normal ring</span><code>P000101</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> Under the finite-minimal-primes and reducedness assumptions, the total ring of fractions is the product of the fraction fields of the minimal components.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/030C\">Stacks 030C · normality criterion with finitely many minimal primes</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-030C\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000421\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000421\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000422":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Normal ring ∧ Finitely many minimal primes ⇒ Finite product of normal domains</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000101\"><span>Normal ring</span><code>P000101</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000092\"><span>Finitely many minimal primes</span><code>P000092</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000422\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000422</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000104\"><span>Finite product of normal domains</span><code>P000104</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> Normality supplies reducedness, so the finite-minimal-primes decomposition applies.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00GV\">Stacks 00GV · normal rings</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00GV\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/030C\">Stacks 030C · finite product criterion</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-030C\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000422\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000422\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000423":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Finite product of normal domains ⇒ Normal ring ∧ Finitely many minimal primes</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000104\"><span>Finite product of normal domains</span><code>P000104</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000423\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000423</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000101\"><span>Normal ring</span><code>P000101</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000092\"><span>Finitely many minimal primes</span><code>P000092</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> Prime ideals of a finite product come from its factors, and localization selects one factor. Each domain factor has one minimal prime.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0CYA\">Stacks 0CYA · finite products of normal rings</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-0CYA\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00EE\">Stacks 00EE · spectra of products</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00EE\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000423\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000423\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000424":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Valuation ring ⇒ Normal domain</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000106\"><span>Valuation ring</span><code>P000106</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000424\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000424</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000102\"><span>Normal domain</span><code>P000102</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> If a nonzero integral element lies outside a valuation ring, its inverse lies in the maximal ideal. Multiplying its monic equation by an inverse power contradicts reduction modulo the maximal ideal.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00IC\">Stacks 00IC · valuation rings are normal domains</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00IC\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000424\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000424\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000425":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"equivalence\" data-relation-style=\"equivalence\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Equivalence</span><h3>Local ring ∧ Integral domain ∧ Every finitely generated ideal is principal ⇔ Valuation ring</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Left conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000080\"><span>Local ring</span><code>P000080</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000078\"><span>Integral domain</span><code>P000078</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000107\"><span>Every finitely generated ideal is principal</span><code>P000107</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000425\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000425</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇔</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Right conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000106\"><span>Valuation ring</span><code>P000106</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> In a local domain with each two-generated ideal principal, a generating pair for a principal ideal contains a generator. Hence the two elements are comparable for divisibility. The valuation condition conversely orders principal ideals.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/090Q\">Stacks 090Q · local domain with principal finitely generated ideals</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-090Q\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00IB\">Stacks 00IB · fraction-field criterion</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00IB\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000425\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000425\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000426":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"equivalence\" data-relation-style=\"equivalence\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Equivalence</span><h3>Every prime ideal is principal ⇔ Every ideal is principal</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Left conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000109\"><span>Every prime ideal is principal</span><code>P000109</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000426\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000426</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇔</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Right conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000108\"><span>Every ideal is principal</span><code>P000108</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> A maximal nonprincipal ideal is prime. If all ideals are principal the implication in the other direction is immediate.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/05KH\">Stacks 05KH · principal-prime-ideal criterion</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-05KH\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000426\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000426\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000427":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Every ideal is principal ⇒ Every finitely generated ideal is principal ∧ Noetherian ring</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000108\"><span>Every ideal is principal</span><code>P000108</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000427\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000427</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000107\"><span>Every finitely generated ideal is principal</span><code>P000107</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000088\"><span>Noetherian ring</span><code>P000088</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> A principal ideal has a finite generating set consisting of at most one element.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00FM\">Stacks 00FM · Noetherian ring</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00FM\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/034U\">Stacks 034U · principal ideals in a PID</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-034U\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000427\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000427\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000428":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Every prime ideal is principal ⇒ Every height-one prime is principal</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000109\"><span>Every prime ideal is principal</span><code>P000109</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000428\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000428</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000110\"><span>Every height-one prime is principal</span><code>P000110</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> The height-one prime ideals form a subset of all prime ideals.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/05KH\">Stacks 05KH · principal prime ideals</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-05KH\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0AFT\">Stacks 0AFT · height-one prime criterion</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-0AFT\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000428\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000428\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000429":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"equivalence\" data-relation-style=\"equivalence\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Equivalence</span><h3>Integral domain ∧ Every ideal is principal ⇔ Principal ideal domain</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Left conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000078\"><span>Integral domain</span><code>P000078</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000108\"><span>Every ideal is principal</span><code>P000108</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000429\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000429</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇔</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Right conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000112\"><span>Principal ideal domain</span><code>P000112</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> This is the definition of a principal ideal domain.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/034U\">Stacks 034U · principal ideal domain</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-034U\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000429\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000429\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000430":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Euclidean domain ⇒ Principal ideal domain</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000113\"><span>Euclidean domain</span><code>P000113</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000430\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000430</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000112\"><span>Principal ideal domain</span><code>P000112</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> In a nonzero ideal choose a nonzero element of least Euclidean value. The remainder of any element of the ideal upon division by it lies in the ideal and must vanish.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00B6\">Stacks 00B6 · Euclidean domain</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00B6\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/034U\">Stacks 034U · principal ideal domain</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-034U\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000430\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000430\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000431":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Principal ideal domain ⇒ Unique factorization domain</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000112\"><span>Principal ideal domain</span><code>P000112</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000431\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000431</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000111\"><span>Unique factorization domain</span><code>P000111</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> A PID is Noetherian and has principal height-one primes, so the height-one criterion applies.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/034V\">Stacks 034V · PID implies UFD</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-034V\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0AFT\">Stacks 0AFT · height-one criterion</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-0AFT\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000431\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000431\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000432":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Principal ideal domain ⇒ Dedekind domain</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000112\"><span>Principal ideal domain</span><code>P000112</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000432\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000432</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000115\"><span>Dedekind domain</span><code>P000115</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> A principal nonzero ideal factors uniquely because a generator factors uniquely into irreducibles, which generate nonzero prime ideals.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0AUQ\">Stacks 0AUQ · PID implies Dedekind</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-0AUQ\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000432\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000432\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000433":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Unique factorization domain ⇒ Normal domain</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000111\"><span>Unique factorization domain</span><code>P000111</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000433\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000433</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000102\"><span>Normal domain</span><code>P000102</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> For a fraction in lowest terms, any irreducible factor of its denominator would divide its numerator if the fraction satisfied a monic equation.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0AFV\">Stacks 0AFV · UFD implies normal domain</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-0AFV\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000433\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000433\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000434":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Noetherian ring ∧ Unique factorization domain ⇒ Every height-one prime is principal</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000088\"><span>Noetherian ring</span><code>P000088</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000111\"><span>Unique factorization domain</span><code>P000111</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000434\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000434</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000110\"><span>Every height-one prime is principal</span><code>P000110</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> For a nonzero element of a height-one prime, one irreducible factor belongs to that prime. Its principal prime ideal must equal the height-one prime.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0AFT\">Stacks 0AFT · height-one criterion</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-0AFT\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000434\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000434\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000435":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Noetherian ring ∧ Integral domain ∧ Every height-one prime is principal ⇒ Unique factorization domain</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000088\"><span>Noetherian ring</span><code>P000088</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000078\"><span>Integral domain</span><code>P000078</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000110\"><span>Every height-one prime is principal</span><code>P000110</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000435\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000435</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000111\"><span>Unique factorization domain</span><code>P000111</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> The ascending chain condition supplies factorization. Principal height-one primes force each irreducible to generate a prime ideal.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0AFT\">Stacks 0AFT · height-one criterion</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-0AFT\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000435\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000435\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000436":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"equivalence\" data-relation-style=\"equivalence\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Equivalence</span><h3>Noetherian ring ∧ Normal domain ∧ Krull dimension at most one ⇔ Dedekind domain</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Left conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000088\"><span>Noetherian ring</span><code>P000088</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000102\"><span>Normal domain</span><code>P000102</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000095\"><span>Krull dimension at most one</span><code>P000095</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000436\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000436</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇔</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Right conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000115\"><span>Dedekind domain</span><code>P000115</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> The Dedekind criterion gives this equivalence, including fields under the stated definition.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/034X\">Stacks 034X · Dedekind-domain criteria</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-034X\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000436\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000436\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000437":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"equivalence\" data-relation-style=\"equivalence\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Equivalence</span><h3>Regular local ring ∧ Krull dimension exactly one ⇔ Discrete valuation ring</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Left conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000117\"><span>Regular local ring</span><code>P000117</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000096\"><span>Krull dimension exactly one</span><code>P000096</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000437\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000437</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇔</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Right conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000114\"><span>Discrete valuation ring</span><code>P000114</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> In dimension one a regular local ring is a domain and its maximal ideal is principal and nonzero. Conversely a discrete valuation ring has Krull dimension one and maximal ideal generated by a uniformizer.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00II\">Stacks 00II · Noetherian valuation rings</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00II\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00PD\">Stacks 00PD · equivalent conditions for a discrete valuation ring</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00PD\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000437\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000437\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000438":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"equivalence\" data-relation-style=\"equivalence\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Equivalence</span><h3>Noetherian ring ∧ Local ring ∧ Normal domain ∧ Krull dimension exactly one ⇔ Discrete valuation ring</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Left conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000088\"><span>Noetherian ring</span><code>P000088</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000080\"><span>Local ring</span><code>P000080</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000102\"><span>Normal domain</span><code>P000102</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000096\"><span>Krull dimension exactly one</span><code>P000096</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000438\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000438</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇔</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Right conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000114\"><span>Discrete valuation ring</span><code>P000114</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> A one-dimensional Noetherian local normal domain is a discrete valuation ring, and a DVR has all four conditions.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00II\">Stacks 00II · Noetherian valuation rings</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00II\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00PD\">Stacks 00PD · equivalent conditions for a discrete valuation ring</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00PD\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000438\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000438\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000439":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Discrete valuation ring ⇒ Valuation ring ∧ Principal ideal domain</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000114\"><span>Discrete valuation ring</span><code>P000114</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000439\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000439</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000106\"><span>Valuation ring</span><code>P000106</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000112\"><span>Principal ideal domain</span><code>P000112</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> The valuation orders all ideals by their integer values. Each nonzero ideal has a generator of the least value that occurs in that ideal.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00II\">Stacks 00II · Noetherian valuation rings</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00II\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00PD\">Stacks 00PD · equivalent conditions for a discrete valuation ring</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00PD\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000439\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000439\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000440":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Local ring ∧ Dedekind domain ∧ Krull dimension exactly one ⇒ Discrete valuation ring</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000080\"><span>Local ring</span><code>P000080</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000115\"><span>Dedekind domain</span><code>P000115</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000096\"><span>Krull dimension exactly one</span><code>P000096</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000440\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000440</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000114\"><span>Discrete valuation ring</span><code>P000114</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> The ring is a Noetherian normal domain and the dimension-one DVR criterion applies.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/034X\">Stacks 034X · Dedekind-domain criteria</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-034X\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00II\">Stacks 00II · Noetherian valuation rings</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00II\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00PD\">Stacks 00PD · equivalent conditions for a discrete valuation ring</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00PD\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000440\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000440\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000441":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Regular ring ⇒ Noetherian ring</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000116\"><span>Regular ring</span><code>P000116</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000441\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000441</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000088\"><span>Noetherian ring</span><code>P000088</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> Noetherianity is part of the definition of a regular ring.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00OD\">Stacks 00OD · regular rings</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00OD\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000441\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000441\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000442":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"equivalence\" data-relation-style=\"equivalence\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Equivalence</span><h3>Regular ring ∧ Local ring ⇔ Regular local ring</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Left conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000116\"><span>Regular ring</span><code>P000116</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000080\"><span>Local ring</span><code>P000080</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000442\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000442</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇔</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Right conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000117\"><span>Regular local ring</span><code>P000117</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> A regular local ring remains regular after localization at every prime. Conversely use regularity at the unique maximal ideal.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00KU\">Stacks 00KU · regular local ring</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00KU\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0AFS\">Stacks 0AFS · localizations of regular local rings</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-0AFS\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00OD\">Stacks 00OD · regular rings</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00OD\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000442\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000442\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000443":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Regular local ring ⇒ Integral domain</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000117\"><span>Regular local ring</span><code>P000117</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000443\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000443</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000078\"><span>Integral domain</span><code>P000078</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> The associated graded ring of the maximal ideal is polynomial over the residue field, and the maximal ideal filtration is separated.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00NP\">Stacks 00NP · regular local rings are domains</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00NP\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000443\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000443\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000444":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Regular local ring ⇒ Unique factorization domain</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000117\"><span>Regular local ring</span><code>P000117</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000444\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000444</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000111\"><span>Unique factorization domain</span><code>P000111</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> Apply the theorem that a regular local ring is factorial.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0AG0\">Stacks 0AG0 · regular local rings are UFDs</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/more-algebra/#tag-0AG0\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000444\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000444\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000445":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Regular ring ⇒ Normal ring</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000116\"><span>Regular ring</span><code>P000116</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000445\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000445</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000101\"><span>Normal ring</span><code>P000101</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> Regularity implies Serre (R₁), and the Cohen–Macaulay local rings imply (S₂). Apply the normality criterion.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0567\">Stacks 0567 · regular rings are normal</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-0567\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000445\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000445\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000446":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"equivalence\" data-relation-style=\"equivalence\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Equivalence</span><h3>Regular ring ∧ Finite Krull dimension ⇔ Noetherian ring ∧ Finite global dimension</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Left conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000116\"><span>Regular ring</span><code>P000116</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000097\"><span>Finite Krull dimension</span><code>P000097</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000446\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000446</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇔</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Right conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000088\"><span>Noetherian ring</span><code>P000088</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000098\"><span>Finite global dimension</span><code>P000098</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> In the Noetherian setting finite global dimension is equivalent to finite-dimensional regularity.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00OE\">Stacks 00OE · finite global dimension and finite dimensional regularity</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00OE\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000446\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000446\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000447":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Regular ring ⇒ Gorenstein ring</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000116\"><span>Regular ring</span><code>P000116</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000447\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000447</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000120\"><span>Gorenstein ring</span><code>P000120</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> Each prime localization is regular local and its self-module in degree zero is a dualizing complex.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0AWX\">Stacks 0AWX · regular rings are Gorenstein</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/dualizing/#tag-0AWX\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000447\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000447\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000448":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"equivalence\" data-relation-style=\"equivalence\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Equivalence</span><h3>Local ring ∧ Gorenstein ring ⇔ Gorenstein local ring</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Left conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000080\"><span>Local ring</span><code>P000080</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000120\"><span>Gorenstein ring</span><code>P000120</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000448\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000448</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇔</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Right conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000121\"><span>Gorenstein local ring</span><code>P000121</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> The local direction uses the unique maximal prime. The converse follows by localization of a dualizing complex.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0DW7\">Stacks 0DW7 · global and local Gorenstein definitions</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/dualizing/#tag-0DW7\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000448\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000448\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000449":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Gorenstein ring ⇒ Cohen–Macaulay ring</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000120\"><span>Gorenstein ring</span><code>P000120</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000449\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000449</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000118\"><span>Cohen–Macaulay ring</span><code>P000118</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> The dualizing complex of a Gorenstein local ring is concentrated in one degree, yielding the Cohen–Macaulay property.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0DW8\">Stacks 0DW8 · Gorenstein rings are Cohen–Macaulay</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/dualizing/#tag-0DW8\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000449\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000449\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000450":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"equivalence\" data-relation-style=\"equivalence\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Equivalence</span><h3>Local ring ∧ Cohen–Macaulay ring ⇔ Cohen–Macaulay local ring</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Left conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000080\"><span>Local ring</span><code>P000080</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000118\"><span>Cohen–Macaulay ring</span><code>P000118</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000450\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000450</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇔</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Right conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000119\"><span>Cohen–Macaulay local ring</span><code>P000119</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> Localize a Cohen–Macaulay local ring at each prime for the global condition. The other direction uses the maximal ideal.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00N8\">Stacks 00N8 · Cohen–Macaulay local ring</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00N8\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00NB\">Stacks 00NB · Cohen–Macaulay localization</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00NB\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00NC\">Stacks 00NC · Cohen–Macaulay ring</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00NC\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000450\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000450\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000451":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Cohen–Macaulay ring ⇒ Universally catenary ring</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000118\"><span>Cohen–Macaulay ring</span><code>P000118</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000451\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000451</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000100\"><span>Universally catenary ring</span><code>P000100</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> Polynomial algebras over the ring are Cohen–Macaulay and hence catenary. Catenarity descends to their quotients.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00NM\">Stacks 00NM · Cohen–Macaulay rings are universally catenary</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00NM\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000451\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000451\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000452":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Complete local ring ⇒ Henselian local ring</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000122\"><span>Complete local ring</span><code>P000122</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000452\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000452</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000123\"><span>Henselian local ring</span><code>P000123</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> Hensel lifting converges in the maximal ideal adic topology.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/04GM\">Stacks 04GM · complete local rings are henselian</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-04GM\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000452\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000452\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000453":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Henselian local ring ⇒ Local ring</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000123\"><span>Henselian local ring</span><code>P000123</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000453\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000453</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000080\"><span>Local ring</span><code>P000080</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> The henselian condition is imposed on a local ring.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/04GF\">Stacks 04GF · henselian and strictly henselian local rings</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-04GF\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000453\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000453\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000454":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Local ring ∧ Krull dimension at most zero ⇒ Henselian local ring</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000080\"><span>Local ring</span><code>P000080</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000094\"><span>Krull dimension at most zero</span><code>P000094</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000454\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000454</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000123\"><span>Henselian local ring</span><code>P000123</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> Finite algebras over the ring have only finitely many primes, all maximal. Their local-product decomposition is the finite-algebra criterion for henselianity.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/06RS\">Stacks 06RS · dimension-zero local rings are henselian</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-06RS\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/04GG\">Stacks 04GG · finite-algebra criterion</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-04GG\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000454\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000454\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000455":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Local ring ∧ Artinian ring ⇒ Complete local ring</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000080\"><span>Local ring</span><code>P000080</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000089\"><span>Artinian ring</span><code>P000089</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000455\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000455</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000122\"><span>Complete local ring</span><code>P000122</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> The maximal ideal is the Jacobson radical, which is nilpotent in an Artinian ring. The system of quotients by its powers is eventually constant.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00J8\">Stacks 00J8 · nilpotent radical</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00J8\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0324\">Stacks 0324 · complete local ring</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-0324\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000455\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000455\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000456":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"equivalence\" data-relation-style=\"equivalence\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Equivalence</span><h3>Henselian local ring ∧ Separably closed residue field ⇔ Strictly henselian local ring</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Left conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000123\"><span>Henselian local ring</span><code>P000123</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000125\"><span>Separably closed residue field</span><code>P000125</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000456\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000456</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇔</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Right conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000124\"><span>Strictly henselian local ring</span><code>P000124</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> This is the definition of strictly henselian.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/04GF\">Stacks 04GF · henselian and strictly henselian local rings</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-04GF\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000456\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000456\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000457":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Local ring ∧ Normal ring ⇒ Geometrically unibranch local ring</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000080\"><span>Local ring</span><code>P000080</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000101\"><span>Normal ring</span><code>P000101</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000457\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000457</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000127\"><span>Geometrically unibranch local ring</span><code>P000127</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> A normal local ring is a normal domain. Its normalization is itself, and its residue field extension is the identity.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00GV\">Stacks 00GV · normal rings</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00GV\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0BPZ\">Stacks 0BPZ · geometrically unibranch local rings</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/more-algebra/#tag-0BPZ\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000457\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000457\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000458":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Geometrically unibranch local ring ⇒ Unibranch local ring</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000127\"><span>Geometrically unibranch local ring</span><code>P000127</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000458\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000458</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000126\"><span>Unibranch local ring</span><code>P000126</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> Geometric unibranchness adds a purely inseparable residue-field condition to unibranchness.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0BPZ\">Stacks 0BPZ · unibranch and geometrically unibranch local rings</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/more-algebra/#tag-0BPZ\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000458\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000458\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000459":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Unibranch local ring ⇒ Local ring ∧ Reduction is a domain</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000126\"><span>Unibranch local ring</span><code>P000126</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000459\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000459</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000080\"><span>Local ring</span><code>P000080</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000128\"><span>Reduction is a domain</span><code>P000128</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> Locality and a domain reduction are two of the defining conditions.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0BPZ\">Stacks 0BPZ · unibranch local rings</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/more-algebra/#tag-0BPZ\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000459\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000459\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000460":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Serre condition (R₁) ⇒ Serre condition (R₀)</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000130\"><span>Serre condition (R₁)</span><code>P000130</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000460\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000460</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000129\"><span>Serre condition (R₀)</span><code>P000129</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> The height-zero primes are included among primes of height at most one.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/031P\">Stacks 031P · Serre conditions for rings and modules</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-031P\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000460\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000460\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000461":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Serre condition (S₂) ⇒ Serre condition (S₁)</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000132\"><span>Serre condition (S₂)</span><code>P000132</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000461\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000461</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000131\"><span>Serre condition (S₁)</span><code>P000131</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> The minimum of two and the local dimension is at least the minimum of one and that dimension.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/031P\">Stacks 031P · Serre conditions for rings and modules</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-031P\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000461\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000461\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000462":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Regular ring ⇒ Serre condition (R₁)</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000116\"><span>Regular ring</span><code>P000116</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000462\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000462</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000130\"><span>Serre condition (R₁)</span><code>P000130</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> The regular ring condition covers every prime, including all primes of height at most one.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00OD\">Stacks 00OD · regular rings</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00OD\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/031P\">Stacks 031P · Serre conditions for rings and modules</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-031P\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000462\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000462\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000463":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Cohen–Macaulay ring ⇒ Serre condition (S₂)</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000118\"><span>Cohen–Macaulay ring</span><code>P000118</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000463\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000463</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000132\"><span>Serre condition (S₂)</span><code>P000132</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> Depth equal to dimension satisfies every finite Serre depth bound.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00NC\">Stacks 00NC · Cohen–Macaulay ring</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00NC\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/031P\">Stacks 031P · Serre conditions for rings and modules</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-031P\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000463\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000463\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000464":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"equivalence\" data-relation-style=\"equivalence\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Equivalence</span><h3>Serre condition (S₁) ⇔ No embedded associated primes</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Left conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000131\"><span>Serre condition (S₁)</span><code>P000131</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000464\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000464</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇔</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Right conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000133\"><span>No embedded associated primes</span><code>P000133</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> Use the associated-prime criterion for Serre (S₁) over a Noetherian ring.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/031Q\">Stacks 031Q · Serre (S₁) and associated primes</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-031Q\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000464\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000464\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000465":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"equivalence\" data-relation-style=\"equivalence\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Equivalence</span><h3>Serre condition (R₀) ∧ Serre condition (S₁) ⇔ Noetherian ring ∧ Reduced ring</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Left conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000129\"><span>Serre condition (R₀)</span><code>P000129</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000131\"><span>Serre condition (S₁)</span><code>P000131</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000465\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000465</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇔</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Right conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000088\"><span>Noetherian ring</span><code>P000088</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000079\"><span>Reduced ring</span><code>P000079</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> The two Serre conditions and the reducedness criterion are stated for Noetherian rings. The Noetherian premise appears explicitly on the reduced side.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/031R\">Stacks 031R · Serre criterion for reducedness</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-031R\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000465\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000465\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000466":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"equivalence\" data-relation-style=\"equivalence\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Equivalence</span><h3>Serre condition (R₁) ∧ Serre condition (S₂) ⇔ Noetherian ring ∧ Normal ring</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Left conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000130\"><span>Serre condition (R₁)</span><code>P000130</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000132\"><span>Serre condition (S₂)</span><code>P000132</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000466\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000466</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇔</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Right conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000088\"><span>Noetherian ring</span><code>P000088</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000101\"><span>Normal ring</span><code>P000101</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> Serre’s normality criterion applies to a Noetherian ring. The Noetherian premise appears explicitly on the normal side.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/031S\">Stacks 031S · Serre criterion for normality</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-031S\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000466\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000466\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000467":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"equivalence\" data-relation-style=\"equivalence\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Equivalence</span><h3>Noetherian ring ∧ Normal ring ∧ Krull dimension at most one ⇔ Regular ring ∧ Krull dimension at most one</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Left conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000088\"><span>Noetherian ring</span><code>P000088</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000101\"><span>Normal ring</span><code>P000101</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000095\"><span>Krull dimension at most one</span><code>P000095</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000467\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000467</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇔</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Right conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000116\"><span>Regular ring</span><code>P000116</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000095\"><span>Krull dimension at most one</span><code>P000095</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> In dimension at most one the (R₁) condition covers every prime. For the converse, a regular ring is Noetherian and normal.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/031S\">Stacks 031S · Serre criterion for normality</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-031S\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0567\">Stacks 0567 · regular rings are normal</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-0567\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00OD\">Stacks 00OD · regular rings</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00OD\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000467\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000467\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000468":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Condition J-2 ⇒ Condition J-1</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000136\"><span>Condition J-2</span><code>P000136</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000468\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000468</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000135\"><span>Condition J-1</span><code>P000135</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> The ring is a finite type algebra over itself.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07P7\">Stacks 07P7 · J-0, J-1 and J-2</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/more-algebra/#tag-07P7\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000468\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000468\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000469":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Condition J-0 ⇒ Noetherian ring ∧ Nonzero ring</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000134\"><span>Condition J-0</span><code>P000134</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000469\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000469</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000088\"><span>Noetherian ring</span><code>P000088</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000076\"><span>Nonzero ring</span><code>P000076</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> The predicate is defined for Noetherian rings and its regular locus contains a prime.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07P7\">Stacks 07P7 · J-0, J-1 and J-2</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/more-algebra/#tag-07P7\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00AQ\">Stacks 00AQ · zero-ring convention</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00AQ\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000469\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000469\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000470":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Regular ring ⇒ Condition J-1</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000116\"><span>Regular ring</span><code>P000116</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000470\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000470</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000135\"><span>Condition J-1</span><code>P000135</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> The entire prime spectrum is the regular locus.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00OD\">Stacks 00OD · regular rings</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00OD\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07P7\">Stacks 07P7 · J-0, J-1 and J-2</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/more-algebra/#tag-07P7\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000470\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000470\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000471":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Regular ring ∧ Nonzero ring ⇒ Condition J-0</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000116\"><span>Regular ring</span><code>P000116</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000076\"><span>Nonzero ring</span><code>P000076</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000471\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000471</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000134\"><span>Condition J-0</span><code>P000134</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> The entire spectrum is regular and is nonempty for a nonzero ring.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00OD\">Stacks 00OD · regular rings</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00OD\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07P7\">Stacks 07P7 · J-0, J-1 and J-2</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/more-algebra/#tag-07P7\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00AQ\">Stacks 00AQ · zero-ring convention</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00AQ\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000471\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000471\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000472":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"equivalence\" data-relation-style=\"equivalence\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Equivalence</span><h3>Noetherian ring ∧ G-ring ∧ Condition J-2 ⇔ Quasi-excellent ring</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Left conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000088\"><span>Noetherian ring</span><code>P000088</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000137\"><span>G-ring</span><code>P000137</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000136\"><span>Condition J-2</span><code>P000136</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000472\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000472</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇔</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Right conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000138\"><span>Quasi-excellent ring</span><code>P000138</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> The three hypotheses are the defining conditions of quasi-excellence.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07QT\">Stacks 07QT · quasi-excellence and excellence</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/more-algebra/#tag-07QT\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000472\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000472\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000473":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"equivalence\" data-relation-style=\"equivalence\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Equivalence</span><h3>Quasi-excellent ring ∧ Universally catenary ring ⇔ Excellent ring</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Left conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000138\"><span>Quasi-excellent ring</span><code>P000138</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000100\"><span>Universally catenary ring</span><code>P000100</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000473\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000473</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇔</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Right conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000139\"><span>Excellent ring</span><code>P000139</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> The two hypotheses are the defining conditions of excellence.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07QT\">Stacks 07QT · quasi-excellence and excellence</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/more-algebra/#tag-07QT\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000473\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000473\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000474":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Quasi-excellent ring ⇒ Nagata ring</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000138\"><span>Quasi-excellent ring</span><code>P000138</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000474\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000474</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000140\"><span>Nagata ring</span><code>P000140</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> Apply the finiteness theorem for normalization over quasi-excellent rings.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07QV\">Stacks 07QV · quasi-excellent rings are Nagata</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/more-algebra/#tag-07QV\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000474\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000474\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000475":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"equivalence\" data-relation-style=\"equivalence\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Equivalence</span><h3>Noetherian ring ∧ Universally Japanese ring ⇔ Nagata ring</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Left conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000088\"><span>Noetherian ring</span><code>P000088</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000141\"><span>Universally Japanese ring</span><code>P000141</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000475\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000475</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇔</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Right conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000140\"><span>Nagata ring</span><code>P000140</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> A Noetherian universally Japanese ring is Nagata by the definitions, and the converse is the Nagata theorem.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/032R\">Stacks 032R · definitions</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-032R\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0334\">Stacks 0334 · Nagata rings are universally Japanese</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-0334\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000475\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000475\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000476":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Noetherian ring ∧ Complete local ring ⇒ Nagata ring</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000088\"><span>Noetherian ring</span><code>P000088</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000122\"><span>Complete local ring</span><code>P000122</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000476\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000476</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000140\"><span>Nagata ring</span><code>P000140</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Commutative rings</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> The theorem on complete local Noetherian rings gives the requisite finiteness for every prime quotient and finite field extension.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/032W\">Stacks 032W · complete local Noetherian rings are Nagata</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-032W\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000476\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000476\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000477":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"equivalence\" data-relation-style=\"equivalence\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Equivalence</span><h3>Finitely presented module ⇔ Maps from the module commute with filtered colimits</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Left conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000143\"><span>Finitely presented module</span><code>P000143</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000477\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000477</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇔</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Right conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000316\"><span>Maps from the module commute with filtered colimits</span><code>P000316</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Modules</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> Finite generators and relations are exactly the condition that the represented Hom functor preserve filtered colimits.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0G8P\">Stacks 0G8P · original statement</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-0G8P\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000477\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000477\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000478":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"equivalence\" data-relation-style=\"equivalence\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Equivalence</span><h3>Projective module ⇔ Module with vanishing first Ext out of it</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Left conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000149\"><span>Projective module</span><code>P000149</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000478\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000478</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇔</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Right conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000318\"><span>Module with vanishing first Ext out of it</span><code>P000318</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Modules</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> The first Ext group vanishes for projective modules, and its vanishing against every module splits any short exact sequence ending in the module.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/05CF\">Stacks 05CF · original statement</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-05CF\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000478\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000478\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000479":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"equivalence\" data-relation-style=\"equivalence\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Equivalence</span><h3>Flat module ⇔ Module with vanishing first Tor against every module</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Left conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000153\"><span>Flat module</span><code>P000153</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000479\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000479</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇔</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Right conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000319\"><span>Module with vanishing first Tor against every module</span><code>P000319</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Modules</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> Flatness gives vanishing of the derived functors of tensor. Conversely, the Tor exact sequence gives the injectivity of tensoring any inclusion.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00M0\">Stacks 00M0 · original statement</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00M0\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00HL\">Stacks 00HL · original statement</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00HL\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00HD\">Stacks 00HD · original statement</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00HD\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000479\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000479\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000480":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Module over a Noetherian ring ∧ Finite module ⇒ Module with finitely many associated primes</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000160\"><span>Module over a Noetherian ring</span><code>P000160</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000142\"><span>Finite module</span><code>P000142</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000480\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000480</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000320\"><span>Module with finitely many associated primes</span><code>P000320</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Modules</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> Use a prime filtration and the inclusion of the associated primes of an extension in the union of those of its terms.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00LC\">Stacks 00LC · original statement</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00LC\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000480\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000480\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000481":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Module of finite projective dimension ⇒ Module of finite Tor dimension</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000173\"><span>Module of finite projective dimension</span><code>P000173</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000481\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000481</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000321\"><span>Module of finite Tor dimension</span><code>P000321</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Modules</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> A finite projective resolution computes derived tensor and is bounded.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00O4\">Stacks 00O4 · original statement</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00O4\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0652\">Stacks 0652 · original statement</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/more-algebra/#tag-0652\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000481\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000481\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000482":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Module of Tor dimension at most d ⇒ Module of finite Tor dimension</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000322\"><span>Module of Tor dimension at most d</span><code>P000322</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000482\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000482</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000321\"><span>Module of finite Tor dimension</span><code>P000321</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Modules</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> The specified interval is one of the bounded intervals permitted in the definition.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0652\">Stacks 0652 · original statement</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/more-algebra/#tag-0652\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000482\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000482\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000483":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"equivalence\" data-relation-style=\"equivalence\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Equivalence</span><h3>Pseudo-coherent module ∧ Module of finite Tor dimension ⇔ Perfect module</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Left conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000175\"><span>Pseudo-coherent module</span><code>P000175</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000321\"><span>Module of finite Tor dimension</span><code>P000321</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000483\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000483</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇔</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Right conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000174\"><span>Perfect module</span><code>P000174</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Modules</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> Apply the perfect complex criterion to the module placed in degree zero.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0658\">Stacks 0658 · original statement</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/more-algebra/#tag-0658\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000483\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000483\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000484":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Quasi-regular ideal ⇒ Ideal with finite projective conormal module</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000199\"><span>Quasi-regular ideal</span><code>P000199</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000484\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000484</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000325\"><span>Ideal with finite projective conormal module</span><code>P000325</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Ideals</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> The degree-one graded piece is free on each principal neighbourhood from the definition. The conormal module is finite locally free over the quotient.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/08RK\">Stacks 08RK · original statement</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/more-algebra/#tag-08RK\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000484\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000484\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000485":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Integral ring map ∧ Injective ring map ⇒ Ring map surjective on spectra</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000206\"><span>Integral ring map</span><code>P000206</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000212\"><span>Injective ring map</span><code>P000212</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000485\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000485</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000217\"><span>Ring map surjective on spectra</span><code>P000217</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Ring homomorphisms</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> Apply lying over to the inclusion of the source as a subring of the target.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00GQ\">Stacks 00GQ · original statement</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00GQ\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000485\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000485\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000486":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Integral ring map ⇒ Going up for prime ideals</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000206\"><span>Integral ring map</span><code>P000206</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000486\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000486</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000336\"><span>Going up for prime ideals</span><code>P000336</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Ring homomorphisms</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> Quotient by the chosen prime over the smaller prime and apply lying over.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00GU\">Stacks 00GU · original statement</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00GU\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000486\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000486\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000487":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Integral ring map ⇒ Incomparability of distinct primes in each fibre</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000206\"><span>Integral ring map</span><code>P000206</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000487\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000487</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000338\"><span>Incomparability of distinct primes in each fibre</span><code>P000338</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Ring homomorphisms</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> The fibre is integral over a field. Every prime in such an algebra is maximal.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00GT\">Stacks 00GT · original statement</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00GT\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000487\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000487\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000488":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Ring map with valuation-ring source ∧ Flat ring map ∧ Ring map of finite type ⇒ Ring map of finite presentation</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000326\"><span>Ring map with valuation-ring source</span><code>P000326</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000208\"><span>Flat ring map</span><code>P000208</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000201\"><span>Ring map of finite type</span><code>P000201</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000488\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000488</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000202\"><span>Ring map of finite presentation</span><code>P000202</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Ring homomorphisms</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> Apply the finite-presentation theorem for flat algebras of finite type over a valuation ring.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/053E\">Stacks 053E · original statement</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/more-algebra/#tag-053E\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000488\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000488\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000489":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Algebra over a perfect field ⇒ Algebra over a field</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000328\"><span>Algebra over a perfect field</span><code>P000328</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000489\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000489</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000327\"><span>Algebra over a field</span><code>P000327</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Ring homomorphisms</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> Being a field is part of the definition.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/030Y\">Stacks 030Y · original statement</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-030Y\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000489\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000489\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000490":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Algebra over a perfect field ∧ Ring map with reduced target ⇒ Geometrically reduced algebra over a field</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000328\"><span>Algebra over a perfect field</span><code>P000328</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000329\"><span>Ring map with reduced target</span><code>P000329</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000490\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000490</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000330\"><span>Geometrically reduced algebra over a field</span><code>P000330</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Ring homomorphisms</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> The geometric reducedness theorem over a perfect field supplies the assertion for all field extensions.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00I4\">Stacks 00I4 · original statement</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00I4\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000490\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000490\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000491":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Geometrically reduced algebra over a field ⇒ Algebra over a field ∧ Ring map with reduced target</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000330\"><span>Geometrically reduced algebra over a field</span><code>P000330</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000491\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000491</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000327\"><span>Algebra over a field</span><code>P000327</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000329\"><span>Ring map with reduced target</span><code>P000329</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Ring homomorphisms</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> Use the trivial field extension to test the target.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/030S\">Stacks 030S · original statement</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-030S\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000491\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000491\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000492":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"equivalence\" data-relation-style=\"equivalence\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Equivalence</span><h3>Geometrically reduced algebra over a field ∧ Geometrically irreducible algebra over a field ⇔ Geometrically integral algebra over a field</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Left conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000330\"><span>Geometrically reduced algebra over a field</span><code>P000330</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000331\"><span>Geometrically irreducible algebra over a field</span><code>P000331</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000492\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000492</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇔</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Right conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000335\"><span>Geometrically integral algebra over a field</span><code>P000335</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Ring homomorphisms</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> Over each extension the ring is a domain exactly when it is reduced with a nonempty irreducible spectrum.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/05DY\">Stacks 05DY · original statement</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-05DY\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000492\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000492\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000493":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Geometrically irreducible algebra over a field ⇒ Geometrically connected algebra over a field</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000331\"><span>Geometrically irreducible algebra over a field</span><code>P000331</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000493\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000493</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000332\"><span>Geometrically connected algebra over a field</span><code>P000332</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Ring homomorphisms</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> A nonempty irreducible space is connected. Apply this after every field extension.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/037T\">Stacks 037T · original statement</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-037T\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/037L\">Stacks 037L · original statement</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-037L\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000493\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000493\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000494":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Geometrically normal algebra over a field ⇒ Geometrically reduced algebra over a field</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000333\"><span>Geometrically normal algebra over a field</span><code>P000333</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000494\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000494</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000330\"><span>Geometrically reduced algebra over a field</span><code>P000330</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Ring homomorphisms</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> Normality of a ring implies reducedness. Apply this after every field extension.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/037Z\">Stacks 037Z · original statement</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-037Z\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/030S\">Stacks 030S · original statement</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-030S\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000494\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000494\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000495":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Geometrically regular Noetherian algebra over a field ⇒ Geometrically normal algebra over a field</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000334\"><span>Geometrically regular Noetherian algebra over a field</span><code>P000334</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000495\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000495</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000333\"><span>Geometrically normal algebra over a field</span><code>P000333</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Ring homomorphisms</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> Regular rings are normal. Test geometric normality after finitely generated field extensions by the equivalent criterion.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0567\">Stacks 0567 · original statement</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-0567\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0381\">Stacks 0381 · original statement</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-0381\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/037Z\">Stacks 037Z · original statement</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-037Z\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000495\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000495\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000496":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Flat ring map ⇒ Going down for prime ideals</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000208\"><span>Flat ring map</span><code>P000208</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000496\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000496</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000337\"><span>Going down for prime ideals</span><code>P000337</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Ring homomorphisms</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> Localize at the given target prime. The induced flat local map is faithfully flat, hence has a surjective map on spectra.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00HS\">Stacks 00HS · original statement</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00HS\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://www.jmilne.org/math/xnotes/CA.pdf#page=49\">J. S. Milne, A Primer of Commutative Algebra, v4.03, Proposition 11.20 (lifting a specialization downwards along a flat homomorphism)</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000496\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000496\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000497":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"equivalence\" data-relation-style=\"equivalence\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Equivalence</span><h3>Étale at a target prime ⇔ Standard étale on a principal neighbourhood</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Left conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000242\"><span>Étale at a target prime</span><code>P000242</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000497\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000497</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇔</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Right conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000339\"><span>Standard étale on a principal neighbourhood</span><code>P000339</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Ring homomorphisms at a prime</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> Every étale map near the chosen point is standard étale after further principal localization. The converse follows from standard étale implying étale.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00UE\">Stacks 00UE · original statement</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00UE\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00UC\">Stacks 00UC · original statement</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00UC\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000497\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000497\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000498":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"equivalence\" data-relation-style=\"equivalence\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Equivalence</span><h3>Finite presentation near a target prime ∧ Flatness at a target prime ∧ Maximal ideal generated from the base at a target prime ∧ Finite separable residue extension at a target prime ⇔ Étale at a target prime</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Left conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000246\"><span>Finite presentation near a target prime</span><code>P000246</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000247\"><span>Flatness at a target prime</span><code>P000247</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000249\"><span>Maximal ideal generated from the base at a target prime</span><code>P000249</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000250\"><span>Finite separable residue extension at a target prime</span><code>P000250</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000498\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000498</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇔</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Right conditions (all)</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000242\"><span>Étale at a target prime</span><code>P000242</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Ring homomorphisms at a prime</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> First restrict to the finitely presented principal neighbourhood, then use the flatness and residue conditions there. Conversely, an étale neighbourhood supplies finite presentation, flatness, and the stated residue conditions.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00U6\">Stacks 00U6 · original statement</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00U6\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00U4\">Stacks 00U4 · original statement</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00U4\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00U2\">Stacks 00U2 · original statement</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00U2\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000498\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000498\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","T000499":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"implies\" data-relation-style=\"implication\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Implication</span><h3>Unramified at a target prime ∧ Chosen point of a finite type ring map ⇒ Quasi-finite at a target prime</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All premises</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000243\"><span>Unramified at a target prime</span><code>P000243</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000340\"><span>Chosen point of a finite type ring map</span><code>P000340</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000499\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">T000499</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⇒</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">All conclusions</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000245\"><span>Quasi-finite at a target prime</span><code>P000245</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Ring homomorphisms at a prime</p><p><strong>Justification.</strong> The finite-type hypothesis allows the quasi-finiteness criterion for the fibre at the chosen prime.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02UR\">Stacks 02UR · original statement</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../topologies/database/stacks/algebra/#tag-02UR\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#T000499\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../topologies/database/#T000499\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>"};
